A||N||10||Dan87GT||Fixing stripped out exhaust manifold bolt hole||||1||
Z||000000||Dan87GT||06-13-2001||07:41 AM||||Hello,<BR>Last summer I repaired the dreaded rear exhaust manifold leak, I stripped one of the bolt holes except a few threads, thought it would hold,it didn't and now its worse than ever. Does anybody have a procedure or company/part#'s for installing helecoils or any other thoughts??? <BR>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||06-13-2001||11:16 AM||||maybe Im wrong on this one (wouldnt be the first time I gave wrong advice on here)<P>my first inclination would be to drill the hole slightly larger and tap it, and put a larger size stud in it - would most likely need to enlarge the hole in the manifold too.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-13-2001||11:20 AM||||BTW if you have never tapped threads into a hole before, the common mistake people make is to screw the tap all the way in.<P> you need to use a cutting oil on it, and you turn it in about one full turn, then you back it off about half a turn to break the chip that is forming, then take another full cutting turn, back it off again, and keep doing that all the way through (down).<P>  otherwise the piece of metal that is being cut off to form the new thread will bind up in there, and the tap will wedge up and seize, maybe even break.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||06-13-2001||03:08 PM||||once you drill and tap it you can get a helicoil, which is basically just a tight spring except instead of the wire beign circular, it looks like the threads on a bolt. you screw the coil in, and then you can use the same sized bolt on the hole, good as new. you should be able to get a 'kit' at most decent parts stores. I have on on my rear inboard manifold, not a problem et all.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Toddster||06-13-2001||03:34 PM||||The biggest mistake people make here is using just a starter tap. There are two types of taps out there. The starter tap is tapered so it goes in easily and cuts the threads slightly deeper with each turn. Once you've reached the base of the tap you need to use a finishing tap which is straight from top to bottom. Otherwise the new bolt will get harder and harder to turn.<BR>Use plenty of red locktite to seat the new helicoil.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||06-13-2001||03:35 PM||||stud stud stud stud stud!!!!!<P>unless you are stranded on a desert island and have no other choice, replace the bolts with studs, always!<P>  it takes about 10 seconds to split the nut off a stud, about an hour to drill out a broken bolt and use an easy out, then drill out the broken easy out...<P>  I know you always think you will never have to take this stupid manifold off ever again.<P>  and about two months later, there you are, taking it off.<BR> <BR>  studs, washers, and soft nuts. Dont even try to unscrew the nuts next time, split them with a chisel and put new ones on. this way the studs will last forever (esp if they are stainless - hint hint (c%||||reg||1
Z||000006||cadaver||06-14-2001||12:51 AM||||I agree use studs when you can on exhaust.<P>There are three styles of taps not two.<P>Isnt it the saturn studs the way to go.||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||06-14-2001||12:03 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>stud stud stud stud stud!!!!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ummm... don't you mean, "Stud, stud, stud, stud, stud, stud?"  ...per head   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'm getting to that point on my GT...thanks for the reminder   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   I got one of two broken exhaust manifold fasteners out - I need to look at using studs all around, test fitting and recording what grip lengths work, and so forth.  Valuable info we don't want to lose track of. <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000008||Toddster||06-14-2001||12:08 PM||||Cadaver, what is the thrid type of tap? And what is its relevance to this problem? We gotta have specifics here.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||06-14-2001||01:28 PM||||um... a beer tap?!<P> if you do get stainless studs, DONT get stainless nuts to go with them - you want soft ones that will split easily cause you will NEVER put a wrench on those nuts - you will ALWAYS take them off with a chisel. <P>  if the chisel torques the nut loose and it unscrews, then unscrew it and throw it away anyway. <P>   too bad there isnt some nut-like fastener you could get that is designed to split off, instead of being unscrewed.<P>  now that i think of it, the numbers stamped into the heads of bolts give an indication of there grade, hardness and strenght. There must be a similar system for nuts too, so you should be able to identify the softess ones in a hardware store.<P>   but please dont ask the clerk, "do you have soft<P>  oh never mind<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||cadaver||06-14-2001||10:36 PM||||as far as taps go there is<BR>a starter or tapered tap. <BR>a plug tap , which has a square end but is still tapered abit.<BR>then a bottoming tap. which cuts the treads to the bottom of the hole.<BR>Most of the time you can use the starter and the bottom but on delicate or exotic stuff you need all three.<BR>Not that it really matters here just posting info.||||reg||1
